[Music]  hello and it's a pleasure to welcome you  to how the light gets ins winter revel  my name is ron amita i teach the history  and politics modern china at oxford  university here in the uk where i'm  sitting at the moment in lockdown and  today i want to talk to you about a  concept which in a sense has become a  bit of a cliche but a cliche which i  think can be  unraveled with actually quite a lot of  useful purpose in understanding one of  the countries in the world which is  making the most waves but sometimes  provokes the least level of  understanding and that is of course  china china the country that is in the  minds of pretty much every person in  every western capital these days  washington dc london berlin wherever it  might be  and whose behavior and his actions  sometimes seem to be in some ways very  hard to understand so getting to the  heart of what really makes up china's  dna is my task in the next half hour or  so i'm going to take you through some  ideas about that let's start though  with this gentleman here probably  familiar to you from the news i hope so  anyway xi jinping general secretary of  the communist party of china president  of the people's republic of china and a  whole variety of other very grand  positions in addition to that now xi  jinping has regularly been in the news  when he turns up at some of the premier  spots  where the west talks to itself and davos  the world economic forum inevitably is  going to be one of those places and i've  got two pictures here  the lower one is of uh xi jinping  speaking in 2017 at davos and uh the one  at the top is actually him speaking at  virtual devils because of course it was  online this year because of the pandemic  both of these talks by xi jinping had  some similarities and both of them had  some differences essentially the one in  2017 is worth remembering because it was  given in the month that donald j trump  was inaugurated as president of the  united states and the second one was  given in the month that donald j trump  left the presidency of the united states  to make way for joe biden  and  in both of these talks the message from  xi jinping about china in the world was  quite similar he talked about china as  being the country that really was  underpinning international order the  country which really people had to pay  attention to if they wanted to support  free and fair trade uh the upholding of  uh broader human values a whole variety  of very warm sounding very positive  sounding elements which i think in both  cases were being formulated by him  without him ever saying so to try and  provide a point of contrast with the  united states  and that contrast perhaps was stronger  in the era of donald trump a president  who decided that he was going to  diverge from the international order  that had become so much part of the  world we've lived in since 1945  and perhaps perhaps not seems less  divergent in the era of a perhaps more  conventional president  but to understand what it was that xi  jinping was trying to say i think during  those talks we have to understand a bit  more about where he  and the chinese communist party and why  the chinese society have been coming  from  for years and decades in some cases even  centuries  now the expression that something is in  someone's dna you know freedom is in  america's dna you know order is in  china's dna whatever just as i said a  bit of a cliche i mean dna you know is  not the right way to think about big  large complex societies in a very  detailed sort of granular way but i  think this idea of something that's  fundamental to them can be useful and  this particular case the reason that i  think that this dna model is a useful  way to think about it is because of what  those elements are  and i'm going to argue during the course  of the next 20 minutes or so that if we  understand four particular factors  authoritarianism  consumerism  globalization  and technology these four  then those  four factors and the way they recombine  with each other tells us something quite  fundamental about why modern china why  contemporary china is the way it is and  the way it's going to be for quite some  time to come and why it's so different  from so many other societies around the  world and those of you who are  biologists or even if you're not  biologists may support it there's  another reason why the dna  metaphor analogy appealed to me so much  those initials a c  g t that you see on screen right now  they are of course also the initials of  the names of the nucleotides the four  different chemicals that make up a  strand of dna so  although the the order acgt is slightly  different in in dna the initials are the  uh the same that's another reason that i  thought this is quite a useful mnemonic  in terms of understanding what the  elements are because  like dna if you take those acgt  nucleotides and put them together dna  makes up so much more than the sum of  its parts it's a very long distance from  looking under an electron microscope a  little speck of dna and seeing a human  being even though of course one is made  up of the other and in the case of china  these four factors are a starting point  but they're not enough  the combination the recombination and  the making of more than some of the  parts is why china is i think so  distinctive  well to understand that i think we need  to think a bit more about the sources of  chinese conduct i use the word conduct  there to echo the essay back in 1947 i  think it was by george kennan the great  american geopolitical thinker about what  he called the sources of soviet conduct  since those days it was the soviet union  not china that was the major  geopolitical opposites to the western  world  and thinking about china today  i think that the chinese communist party  the party state that runs china draws  its legitimacy from a whole variety of  sources the one it doesn't draw from of  course is democratic legitimacy because  china neither has a liberal democratic  system based on elections nor does it  make any pretense certainly these days  of wanting to do so so where does it  draw its sense of strength and  legitimacy from well i say there are  several factors number one is its modern  history the story that china tells about  itself to its own people and that it  tells in the wider world is a story of  repeated invasion and defense against  outside occupation and attack  that starts in the mid 19th century with  the opium wars where the british of  course turn up with gun boats off the  south coast of china  wanting to break open the doors of  china's closed economy as they saw it  selling opium and also bringing along  with them bibles missionaries western  modes of thinking and modernity in  general  and for many chinese certainly at the  time and even now  the century that followed was a very  unhappy one from the 1830s through the  1940s  then and even now sometimes called the  century of humiliation for china as i  say a very unhappy period and this  includes factors or events such as the  occupation of hong kong at the end of  the opium war uh the seeding of parts of  manchuria and northeast china uh in the  1890s and 1900s and eventually of course  the occupation of manchuria by the  japanese in the 1930s  ultimately leading to the outbreak of  full-scale world war ii in asia 1937 is  when that starts in china and the war  goes on for eight years till 1945 when  china finally ends up on the allied side  of the war exhausted but ultimately  victorious  so that whole history of attack  oppression and defense against the  outside is one factor that explains the  highly defensive language that you hear  so often when china chooses to define  itself to the outside world my own take  is that much of this i think is deeply  exaggerated these days china today is a  strong country whereas 100 years ago it  was very much a weak country but that  having been said you can understand  historically one of the reasons why that  sense of oppression from outside remains  a very powerful factor  the second factor is the one that you  tend to come up with most when you deal  with china's place in the world as an  economic business actor which is the  idea that whatever you think of it  china's economic reform certainly since  the late 1970s early 1980s  have provided the source for a huge  increase in uh poverty reduction or  enrichment if you want to put it in a  more positive way in china itself over  the last 40 years  regardless of the methods and the  methods could often be quite brutal and  quite painful you can look at the  statistics and see that from being an  immensely impoverished country in the  1970s china has moved to being as i'm  sure you're aware the second biggest  economy in the world in the 2010s 2020s  that shift did not come from nowhere  so that ability of the chinese system  authoritarian and dictatorial though it  is to provide economic growth and wealth  is a very important pillar of legitimacy  the third one again it's one that's  sometimes unappreciated under  appreciated i think is the uh  dependence on what you might call  traditional thought particularly  thinking of  great chinese philosophers of the past  like confucius  who had a whole variety of ideas that we  still find very congenial today the idea  of a harmonious world being one of them  and other ideas that frankly are  probably not so congenial if you look  into many detail confusions and  traditional confusion thinking was not  about equality but about hierarchy and  in the modern world we don't tend to  think very much about hierarchy as  something that societies might actually  want rather than trying to overcome but  in the case of traditional confusion  thinking  to continue watching this video click  the link in the top left or in the  description below or visit iai dot tv  for more debates and talks from the  world's leading thinkers on today's  biggest ideas

